// parity-expected-stdout-begin
// 3
// parity-expected-stdout-end

// Effect polymorphism: a row variable threaded from a callback to the caller.
//
// `apply_twice` does not say *which* effects `f` performs. It says its own row
// is whatever `f`'s row is, by naming the same variable `|e` in both places.
// A pure callback makes the whole call pure; an effectful one makes the caller
// carry those effects, with no second definition.
//
// Two syntax rules are load-bearing here. A row variable is written `|e`, not
// a bare `e`; and a callback parameter carrying a row must have its function
// type parenthesized unless it is the final parameter.
//
// This works because the signature is written. The unannotated version of the
// same wrapper does not infer — see
// ../../failing/compile/ki_032_unannotated_row_poly_wrapper.flx.

fn apply_twice(f: ((Int) -> Int with |e), x: Int) -> Int with |e {
    f(f(x))
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(apply_twice(\n -> n + 1, 1))
}
